### What Code Was There?
#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
    while(1) {
        FILE *f = fopen("dummy.txt", "a");
        fprintf(f, "Writing I/O data...\n");
        fclose(f);
    }
}
It keeps writing to a file - that's I/O work, not CPU work.

### Viva for 9(b) - Remember This:

*Q1. Difference between 9(a) and 9(b)?*
> 9(a) = CPU busy with `while(1) counter++` - shows 99% CPU in `top`
> 9(b) = I/O busy with file writing - shows very low %CPU, high wait

*Q2. What are `fopen`, `fprintf`, `fclose`?*
> For file handling. fopen = open file, fprintf = write, fclose = close.

*Q3. What is `pgrep -a expt9b`?*
> Finds Process ID (PID) of our running program.

*Q4. What is `pmap -x PID`?*
> Shows memory mapping of that process - how much memory it uses, its libraries. This is the "Memory Mapping" part of title.

*Q5. What is `cat dummy.txt`?*
> To see what was written in the file during I/O task.

*Trick to Remember:*
> If Sir asks "Which uses more CPU?" -> Say "9(a) uses more CPU, 9(b) uses more I/O and CPU is almost free."

